  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### D2. Zero pattern vs the counting law\n\n`.` = entry is exactly zero, `X` = nonzero. Four columns per `beta` (the `(a,b)` choices).\n\n**`{0,1,0}`**, `lambdahat = {0,2}`, `lambdabarhat_3 = 2`, `lambdabarhat_4 = 0`:\n\n| row | beta=1 | beta=2 | alpha=1 law `4-lh_j-lbh_k-beta` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (1,1,3) | `. . . .` | `X X X X` | `2-beta` -> 1,0  ✓ |\n| (1,1,4) | `. . . .` | `. . . .` | `4-beta` -> 3,2  ✓ |\n| (1,2,3) | `X . . X` | `X X X X` | `-beta` -> 0,0  (2 anomalous zeros) |\n| (1,2,4) | `. . . .` | `X X X X` | `2-beta` -> 1,0  ✓ |\n| (2,1,3) | `X X X X` | `X X X X` | -- |\n| (2,1,4) | `X X X X` | `. . . .` | -- |\n| (2,2,3) | `X X X X` | `X X X X` | -- |\n| (2,2,4) | `X X X X` | `X X X X` | -- |\n\n**`{1,2,1}`**, `lambdahat = {1,2}`, `lambdabarhat_3 = 2`, `lambdabarhat_4 = 1`:\n\n| row | beta=1 | beta=2 | alpha=1 law |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (1,1,3) | `X X X X` | `X X X X` | `1-beta` -> 0,0  ✓ |\n| (1,1,4) | `. . . .` | `X X X X` | `2-beta` -> 1,0  ✓ |\n| (1,2,3) | `X . . X` | `X X X X` | `-beta` -> 0,0  (2 anomalous zeros) |\n| (1,2,4) | `X X X X` | `X X X X` | `1-beta` -> 0,0  ✓ |\n| (2,1,3) | `X X X X` | `X X X X` | -- |\n| (2,1,4) | `X . . X` | `X X X X` | -- |\n| (2,2,3) | `X X X X` | `X X X X` | -- |\n| (2,2,4) | `X X X X` | `X X X X` | -- |\n\n**Status of the law.**\n\n1. At `alpha = 1` the law `Max[0, 2L - lambdahat_j - lambdabarhat_k - beta]` predicts the\n   zero pattern correctly for blocks `(1,3)`, `(1,4)`, `(2,4)` in *both* states.\n2. Block `(2,3)` carries two extra zeros in *both* states, always at the mixed shifts\n   `(a,b) = (1,-1)` and `(-1,1)`, i.e. `a = -b`. The law is blind to `(a,b)`, so this is a\n   genuine refinement, not a failure of the degree counting.\n3. `alpha >= 2` is still NOT described. The only `alpha = 2` vanishing is in block `(1,4)`\n   of `{0,1,0}`, and it sits at `beta = 2`, i.e. *increasing* in `beta` -- the opposite\n   ordering to the `alpha = 1` law. A naive shift `2L -> 2L - 2(alpha-1)` predicts the\n   reverse and is wrong. Whatever governs `alpha >= 2` is a different mechanism (the\n   closing-at-infinity argument genuinely only applies at `alpha = 1`, where the factor\n   `Exp[2 Pi u (alpha-1)]` is absent).\n\nNote `{1,2,1}` has no `alpha=1` row fully vanishing, so its min-cost is 0 and `B = 7776`\nis finite; `{0,1,0}` has the whole `(1,1,4)` row zero, so its determinant vanishes and the\nreduced minor is required.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## E. One determinant, not two: the (L1) prefactor absorbs the corank\n\nThe `alpha = 1` bracket in the untwisted limit is\n\n```\n<P>_{L,1} = Integral du/(-2 Pi) P(u) / (u^2 + 1/4)^L\n```\n\nwhich **annihilates every `P` with `deg P < 2L-1`**. So the `alpha=1` rows are functionals\nseeing only the top of the degree filtration, and whether a row is killed depends on\n`lambdahat_j + lambdabarhat_k`. The corank is therefore genuinely state-dependent.\n\nThat is exactly what Appendix L's prefactor compensates. (L1) reads\n\n```\nB = Prod_{j<k} z_jk^(lambda_j - lambda_k) / (Q12(-i/2) Q34(i/2))^(2L) * det<...>\n```\n\nAlong a ray all `z_jk ~ eps`, so the prefactor contributes `eps^Sum_{j<k}(lambda_j-lambda_k)`.\nMeasured against the order of the (12)-determinant:\n\n| state | lambda | Sum_{j<k} lambda_jk | measured det order | total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| {0,1,0} | {0,1,1,2} | -6 | eps^6  (ratios 5.963, 5.991, 5.998, 5.999) | 0 |\n| {1,2,1} | {1,1,1,1} |  0 | eps^0  (B -> 7776) | 0 |\n\nBoth cancel exactly. Hence the closed formula for the overall exponent:\n\n```\nnu = -Sum_{j<k}(lambda_j - lambda_k) = -Sum_j (n+1-2j) lambda_j     (n = 4)\n```\n\n**So there is one determinant, not a different one per state** -- provided one uses (L1)'s\n`B` rather than (12)'s. The state-dependence of the vanishing is precisely the\nstate-dependence of the prefactor.\n\n**Caveat on evaluation.** (L1) makes `B` finite, but when the corank is positive the\nuntwisted determinant is literally `0` while the prefactor is infinite. To *evaluate* the\nfinite answer purely in untwisted Q's one still needs the reduced minor (whose size is the\ncorank) -- as in `SU4_norm_untwisting_L2.wb`, where {0,1,0} needed `det'` and gave 8.\nFor {1,2,1} the corank is 0 and the plain determinant already works (7776).",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  }
